Here’s how to work with `parse_args_with` for an attribute in **syn** 2.0:

## Accessing the Attribute's Path

You access the path **via `attr.meta.path()`**, because `meta` is now a `Meta` enum, and it exposes the path.

```rust
use syn::{Attribute, Meta};

fn is_my_attribute(attr: &Attribute) -> bool {
    matches!(
        attr.meta.require_path().ok().map(|path| path.is_ident("my_attribute")),
        Some(true)
    )
}
```
- Use `attr.meta.require_path()?` to get the path. (All meta items now use the [Meta] type.)

## Parsing Attribute Arguments

To use `parse_args_with`, you need to make sure your attribute uses the form `#[something(...)]`.

```rust
use syn::{Attribute, parse::Parser, Expr};

fn parse_attr_args(attr: &Attribute) -> syn::Result<Expr> {
    attr.parse_args_with(Expr::parse)
}
```
**Key changes in syn 2.0:**
- There's no longer an `attr.tokens` field; instead, use `attr.parse_args_with`.
- The attribute style must support arguments (`#[attr(args)]`).

## Example: Parse Custom Attribute Arguments

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
struct MyArgs {
    name: syn::Ident,
    eq_token: syn::token::Eq,
    value: syn::LitStr,
}

impl syn::parse::Parse for MyArgs {
    fn parse(input: syn::parse::ParseStream) -> syn::Result<Self> {
        Ok(Self {
            name: input.parse()?,
            eq_token: input.parse()?,
            value: input.parse()?,
        })
    }
}

fn parse_my_attribute(attr: &Attribute) -> syn::Result<MyArgs> {
    attr.parse_args_with(MyArgs::parse)
}
```

